    <exercise number="311" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\int_{0}^{2}{\left( {1 - t} \right)\cos \left( {\pi t} \right)dt}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$u=1-t;u=1-t;$$ the integral becomes $$\int 1-1ucos(\pi (1-u))du=\int 1-1u[cos\pi cos\pi u-sin\pi sin\pi u]du=-\int 1-1ucos\pi udu=\int -11ucos\pi udu=0\int 1-1ucos(\pi (1-u))du=\int 1-1u[cos\pi cos\pi u-sin\pi sin\pi u]du=-\int 1-1ucos\pi udu=\int -11ucos\pi udu=0$$ since the integrand is odd.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
