    <exercise number="315" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the area under the graph of $$g(t) = \frac{t}{\left( {1 - t^{2}} \right)^{a}}$$ between $$t = 0$$ and $$t = x,$$ where $$0 &lt; x &lt; 1$$ and $$a &gt; 0$$ is fixed. Evaluate the limit as $$x\rightarrow 1.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\int 0xg(t)dt=12\int u=1-x21duua=12(1-a)u1-a|u=1-x21=12(1-a)(1-(1-x2)1-a).\int 0xg(t)dt=12\int u=1-x21duua=12(1-a)u1-a|u=1-x21=12(1-a)(1-(1-x2)1-a).$$ As $$x\to 1x\to 1$$ the limit is $$12(1-a)12(1-a)$$ if $$a&lt;1,a&lt;1,$$ and the limit diverges to +∞ if $$a\ge 1.a\ge 1.$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="316" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The area of a semicircle of radius 1 can be expressed as $${\int_{-1}^{1}{\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}dx}}.$$ Use the substitution $$x = \cos t$$ to express the area of a semicircle as the integral of a trigonometric function. You do not need to compute the integral.</paragraph>
      </problem>
